WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:02.919
This video is sponsored by Skillshare.

00:00:02.919 --> 00:00:06.970
Use the link in the description to watch my
course or thousands of others with a free

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:09.330
two-month trial.

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:17.030
In 2018, the U.S. imported $540 billion worth
of goods from China, but exported only 120.

00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:23.380
In response, President Trump imposed tariffs,
immediately shaking the Chinese, American,

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:25.850
and, therefore, world, economies.

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:31.500
But while the trade war defines today’s
economy, another deficit will decide tomorrow’s.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:38.429
In the 2017-18 school year, 363,000 Chinese
students came to study in the U.S., while

00:00:38.429 --> 00:00:42.120
only 24,000 Americans went to China.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:44.800
This, on the surface, isn’t all that surprising.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:48.789
What’s weird - really weird - is how fast
it happened.

00:00:48.789 --> 00:00:54.149
All of a sudden, starting in 2007, Chinese
students in the U.S. absolutely exploded,

00:00:54.149 --> 00:00:59.399
accounting for 93% of all international student
growth in the last decade.

00:00:59.399 --> 00:01:04.500
More students come from China to America than
the next six countries combined, including

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:08.010
India, despite having almost the same size
population.

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:10.090
So, why so many?

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:11.290
Why so sudden?

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:14.680
And is the U.S. right to worry about incoming
spies?

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:20.350
The answer has less to do with academics and
more with economics, complex social dynamics,

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:25.310
and, above all, politics.

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:29.259
On June 7th, Chinese cities become… eerily
quiet.

00:01:29.259 --> 00:01:34.580
Traffic is as busy as ever but no horns are
honking, stress is collectively unusually

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:38.869
high, free water bottles are handed out, and
drones watch overhead.

00:01:38.869 --> 00:01:44.840
Today, tomorrow, and sometimes, on a third
day, 10 million students across China take

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:50.960
the National Higher Education Entrance Exam,
aka the Gaokao, aka, the most important nine

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:53.530
hours of a Chinese person’s life.

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:58.350
The test covers Chinese language and literature,
math, foreign language (usually English),

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:01.740
and a choice of social or natural science.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:07.030
The top roughly 7-million scorers are admitted
to college, and a select few are offered places

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:11.009
in the C9 - mainland China’s equivalent
to the Ivy-League.

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:18.360
But unlike the SAT, AP, or IB, Gaokao scores
are really the only factor in Chinese college

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:19.410
admissions.

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:24.230
The first 18 years of your life, therefore,
are dedicated to preparation.

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:29.840
Leading up to the big test, parents burn incense,
pray, and book hotels near the exam to avoid

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:30.940
traffic.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:33.130
Students sometimes study with IV drips.

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:37.590
Some, known as “Gaokao migrants”, travel
to other provinces with higher admissions

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:40.720
quotas in hopes of having a slight advantage.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:45.470
When the day finally comes, provincial governments
order quiet streets for concentration and

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:47.930
fly drones to catch cheaters.

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:53.110
Supporters of the Gaokao say it levels the
playing field - creating a meritocracy wherein

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:58.340
any student, from any geographic or socioeconomic
part of China has the same opportunity for

00:02:58.340 --> 00:02:59.720
social mobility.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:05.450
Critics, in turn, argue a level playing field
is only ever an illusion - that success is

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:09.620
handed to those with families wealthy enough
to afford private tutors.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:14.760
Like continued middle-class growth, the national
exam is both a practical and political tool

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:20.459
for maintaining stability - shifting questions
of who has power and who is entitled to riches

00:03:20.459 --> 00:03:22.439
onto the individual.

00:03:22.439 --> 00:03:27.500
The extreme, sometimes insurmountable stress,
they say, doesn't even produce good citizens

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:29.090
or employees.

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:33.350
While Chinese students rank very highly in
math and science, they’re often seen as

00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:37.630
lacking in other skills like creative and
critical thinking, a side effect of their

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:39.750
rigid education system.

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:44.769
Classrooms are dominated by the teacher, who
lectures behind a podium to a sea of totally-silent

00:03:44.769 --> 00:03:48.010
students expected to memorize as much as possible.

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:52.940
To ask questions is both to disrespect your
teacher and admit to your peers that you don’t

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:54.590
understand the material.

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:59.670
Finally, discipline is placed above all else,
with low performers at one high school not

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:01.530
being allowed air conditioning.

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:06.400
For any number of these reasons, some, disenchanted
parents seek a way out.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:11.010
If their child performs poorly on his or her
high school entrance exam, rather than lose

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:15.200
face, families may place them in international
schools, designed to prepare them for exams

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:17.590
like the SAT, instead.

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:21.820
Others pursue an education abroad with the
intent of eventually migrating the whole family,

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:24.220
or, simply, for more opportunity.

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:29.660
The perception is that, while school in China
is more intense up until the Gaokao, afterward,

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:34.370
students feel they’ve satisfied their family’s
expectations and can relax at university,

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:37.830
whereas American college is when students
start getting serious.

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:42.009
In other words, students leave China on their
parents’ suggestion, who usually pay their

00:04:42.009 --> 00:04:43.009
tuition.

00:04:43.009 --> 00:04:44.220
And pay, do they!

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:50.590
There are English lessons, extracurriculars
for admissions, exam fees, and travel costs.

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:56.280
On top of that, families pay agencies about
$10,000 per child for help in the process.

00:04:56.280 --> 00:05:01.370
In other words, this is only possible thanks
to China’s rising, newly-wealthy middle-class,

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:06.130
and the demographics which leave parents with
only one child to pay for - and, more importantly,

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:08.410
only one chance to get it right.

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:13.030
The truly wealthy get started even earlier
- sending their child to an elite American

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:17.620
feeder middle school, which can charge up
to 60, $70,000 a year.

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:21.979
And when old fashion studying doesn’t work,
upper-class families resort to “gifts”

00:05:21.979 --> 00:05:27.220
- usually about $250,000, and as much as $6.5
million.

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:32.039
There’s one more, unexpected reason Chinese
students come to America…

00:05:32.039 --> 00:05:36.340
When Deng Xiaoping began opening up the country
in the ’80s and ‘90s, creating thousands

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:41.890
of newly rich families, he also, for the first
time, allowed students to study overseas.

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:46.650
For this reason, the first international students
who returned to China were its most well-off,

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:49.810
launching high-paying, high-profile careers.

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:54.250
This association of studying in America and
success in life has never faded.

00:05:54.250 --> 00:06:00.440
So, while the American Dream may not be alive
and well in America, it certainly is in Beijing.

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:03.860
Americans have Louis Vuitton, McMansions,
and Porsche’s.

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:06.280
Chinese people have Harvard and Yale.

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:10.970
One hospital in central China even named its
maternity wards after Ivy-League schools for

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:12.430
good luck.

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:17.979
All of these factors help explain this, but
they don’t justify this.

00:06:17.979 --> 00:06:20.570
Why did it all happen so fast?

00:06:20.570 --> 00:06:25.190
To answer that, we need to understand how
schools really make money.

00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:29.570
Broadly speaking, in the U.S., there are two
university business models.

00:06:29.570 --> 00:06:33.490
The first way a school can make money is simple:
charging students.

00:06:33.490 --> 00:06:37.850
Private schools are the Apple of education
- they forgo massive market share in exchange

00:06:37.850 --> 00:06:40.569
for a smaller number of higher-paying students.

00:06:40.569 --> 00:06:46.490
And, because they attract high-income families,
they can expect good, lifelong customers - aka

00:06:46.490 --> 00:06:47.580
endowments!

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:51.879
On the other hand, the way public schools
pay the bills is a little less obvious.

00:06:51.879 --> 00:06:57.080
Lower tuition is made up for by state and
federal funding - aka, everyone’s favorite,

00:06:57.080 --> 00:06:58.080
taxes!

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:00.930
Government subsidizing is great - when it’s
great.

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:05.250
Low prices grant low-income families access
to a great education.

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:09.770
The problem is that state and federal governments
have other priorities and are subject to economic

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:11.030
downturns.

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:16.180
During the 2008 recession, Americans spent
and made less money, governments collected

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:19.650
less revenue, and colleges received less funding.

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:24.300
From 2008 to 13, states alone lost out on
$283 billion.

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:31.210
Now, ten years later, most of us have long
forgotten the recession - but not universities.

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:37.500
Still in 2018, state funding for higher education
was down 13% from before the crisis.

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:42.840
So, as government subsidies fell, schools
immediately turned to a new subsidy - international

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:43.840
tuition.

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:48.569
The current model is one where colleges can
segment prices without appearing to discriminate.

00:07:48.569 --> 00:07:53.379
In other words, tuition is set very high,
but aid is handed out very generously.

00:07:53.379 --> 00:08:00.139
The average full-time undergraduate in 2017-18
received nearly $15,000 in total aid.

00:08:00.139 --> 00:08:05.570
But while something like 85% of students receive
some amount of financial aid, international

00:08:05.570 --> 00:08:08.440
students almost always pay full price.

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:15.070
At Michigan State University, for example,
in-state freshmen pay $25,064 a year for tuition,

00:08:15.070 --> 00:08:16.970
fees, room, and board.

00:08:16.970 --> 00:08:23.970
Out-of-state residents pay just over double,
and international students pay $9,133 on top

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:25.610
of that.

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:30.050
Across America, an international student generates
about twice as much revenue as an in-state

00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:31.440
resident.

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:36.240
Students also complain about a so-called “International
Tax”, where schools place a greater emphasis

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:39.360
on English courses to prolong their studies.

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:43.720
Increasingly, Chinese students find themselves
caught between two worlds…

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:50.180
As more and more students return home, 30%
in 2007, but 80% today, they’re often disappointed

00:08:50.180 --> 00:08:51.640
by what they find.

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:56.620
While English is still very valuable and many
find high-paying jobs in America, the rest,

00:08:56.620 --> 00:09:00.110
“Haigui”, as they’re known in Chinese,
have a disadvantage.

00:09:00.110 --> 00:09:05.370
One study found U.S. diploma-holders were
18% less likely to receive a call back from

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:08.240
potential employers than Chinese ones.

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:13.340
On the other hand, they may also feel isolated
and unwelcome in America.

00:09:13.340 --> 00:09:18.300
Because schools tend only to keep one or two
dorms open during breaks, during which international

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:22.640
students tend to stay on campus, they get
placed in the same dorms, have less opportunity

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:27.340
to perfect their language skills, and a harder
time socializing outside their bubble.

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:31.890
At the same time, some Chinese students are
experiencing delayed or rejected visas and

00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:34.150
accusations of espionage.

00:09:34.150 --> 00:09:39.340
The fear stems from Confucius Institutes or
Chinese Student and Scholars Associations,

00:09:39.340 --> 00:09:44.610
groups set-up by or associated with China’s
Communist Party on American campuses.

00:09:44.610 --> 00:09:49.290
Officially, their goal is to help Chinese
students acclimate abroad - like, by organizing

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:51.710
parties around Chinese New Year.

00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:56.360
Chinese embassies also create WeChat groups
to organize students, even paying them to

00:09:56.360 --> 00:10:00.750
welcome Xi Jinping during his 2015 visit to
Washington.

00:10:00.750 --> 00:10:04.950
Several Chinese students and faculty have
been arrested or fired in recent years for

00:10:04.950 --> 00:10:08.980
alleged spying or failing to disclose connections
to China.

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:14.400
According to sources, President Trump seriously
considered banning all Chinese students completely,

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:19.180
only narrowly deciding against it after an
ambassador pointed out how it would harm American

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:20.310
schools.

00:10:20.310 --> 00:10:25.380
Students in STEM fields, in other words, most
Chinese students, are already subject to additional

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:26.380
scrutiny.

00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:31.670
The truth is, visa issues are not yet widespread,
and the U.S. government has, at times, even

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:36.810
encouraged Chinese arrivals, with Trump declaring
“We want to have Chinese students (go) to

00:10:36.810 --> 00:10:38.790
our great schools and great universities.

00:10:38.790 --> 00:10:41.410
They are great students and tremendous assets”.

00:10:41.410 --> 00:10:46.730
Regardless, issues are common enough to create
a perception of risk, leading to an 8% drop

00:10:46.730 --> 00:10:52.600
of international students in 2018, who increasingly
choose other countries like Canada or affordable

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:53.610
Thailand.

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:59.670
The University of Illinois went so far as
to take out a $424,000 insurance policy in

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:02.570
case of a significant drop in Chinese students.

00:11:02.570 --> 00:11:07.120
The U.S. can and should be worried about Chinese
influence on campuses.

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:12.010
Their free, open-minded approach has the potentially
dangerous side-effect of also creating a vulnerable

00:11:12.010 --> 00:11:15.550
hole easily filled by nationalist propaganda.

00:11:15.550 --> 00:11:20.370
There has never been a better time in history
to be wary of China’s influence abroad.

00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:25.800
But there has also never been a more important
moment to be cautious about conflating a government

00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:29.890
and its ideology with 1.4 billion individuals.

00:11:29.890 --> 00:11:36.670
Suspecting everyone of espionage leaves America
economically and culturally weaker, not stronger.

00:11:36.670 --> 00:11:41.710
Every year, Chinese students contribute $15
billion to the U.S. economy.

00:11:41.710 --> 00:11:46.690
Education is now Australia’s third-largest
export, more than tourism, and behind only

00:11:46.690 --> 00:11:48.330
iron and coal.

00:11:48.330 --> 00:11:53.490
But whether economically useful or not, cultural
exchanges act as a countervailing force to

00:11:53.490 --> 00:11:58.850
propaganda - both exposing Chinese nationals
to a wider intellectual world and American

00:11:58.850 --> 00:12:01.140
citizens to foreign cultures.

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:06.010
The fact that America has so many high-ranking,
sought-after institutions - where even Xi

00:12:06.010 --> 00:12:11.080
Jinping sends his daughter - is a massive
diplomatic advantage that risks being wasted

00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:13.040
if foreigners aren’t welcome.

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:18.080
When students return to China, these schools
often constitute their entire conception of

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:23.390
America, the one that spreads to friends,
family, and, eventually, decision-makers.

00:12:23.390 --> 00:12:28.100
Cutting off Chinese students may help win
today’s trade war, but welcoming them is

00:12:28.100 --> 00:12:33.240
the only way to stop tomorrow’s conflicts
before they even begin.

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:37.330
While not everyone can leave their country
and study abroad, we all have access to some

00:12:37.330 --> 00:12:40.900
of the most interesting classes online with
